---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Home repair costs 2026: who publishes them and how it works';
const description = 'Home Costs by State is published by Radif Partners: free 2026 home cost calculators built on Census, BLS and EIA data, no lead form and no contractor referral.';
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About Home Costs by State</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>Most pages that answer "how much does a new roof cost" end in a form: a ZIP code, then a name and a phone number, and the price arrives later from a contractor who paid for the contact. This site does better than the first Google result for those searches because it gives the price at once, without a ZIP code or a phone number, from what homeowners actually paid according to the Census Bureau, adjusted to each state with the May 2025 BLS wages of the trade, and it publishes the method and the sources behind every figure.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds calculators on public data. It is not a contractor, a lead service, a retailer, a manufacturer or a utility, and it has no commercial link with any of them. No page sells your request, no contractor is told that you looked, and there is no advertising on the site today.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What we use, and what we refuse to use</h2>
    <p>We use what anyone can download and check: the American Housing Survey 2023 ({K.num(K.ahs('roof').pro.n)} professional roof jobs, {K.num(K.ahs('waterHeater').pro.n)} water heaters, {K.num(K.ahs('centralAc').pro.n)} central air jobs among others), the Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics of May 2025, the 2022 Economic Census, the Energy Information Administration's price series, and the solar data of the national laboratories. We refuse unsourced "average prices" from industry blogs, retailer prices we cannot read reliably, and estimates generated by software we cannot inspect. When a figure does not exist, we say so: there is, for example, no federal survey of heat pump prices on their own, and the <a href={route('heat-pump-cost', lang)}>heat pump page</a> explains what we do instead.</p>
    <h2>How the site is checked</h2>
    <p>Each data file is rebuilt by a script from the official download, and the calculators are tested on known cases before every release. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> gives the formula step by step, with a worked example, and lists what the numbers cannot know. The <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains how corrections are handled.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions, corrections and data suggestions: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, or the <a href={route('contact', lang)}>contact page</a>. We do not recommend contractors and cannot review a quote, but we read every message about a figure.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
